{
  "title": "Canonical row identity for keyed lists (FORMA-181 checkpoint B, DR-8, v2 6.5 and 6.8)",
  "contract": "A list key is a string or a safe integer. A string key is written as N(s) of value rule 1 (normalization.json): CRLF and CR become LF, NUL becomes U+FFFD, nothing else changes. A number key must be an integer within plus or minus 2^53-1 and prints as its decimal integer; -0 prints as 0. null, booleans, arrays, objects, fractions, infinities and NaN are not identities: the native render refuses the page (RENDER-503) and core's canonicalKeyText returns undefined and adoption refuses FORMA_E_HYDRATE_KEY. Two rows of one list execution whose canonical text is equal are duplicates. The path is read against the row's own fields only: every segment but the last names a field holding an object and the last names a field holding an identity; a missing field, a field declared twice or a non-object on the way refuses.",
  "consumers": [
    "crates/forma-ir/src/walker.rs keyed_rows test: every value with `served` renders through the walker as exactly that data-forma-key attribute value (the escaped markup)",
    "packages/core/e2e/keyed-adoption.spec.ts: the `served` markup parsed by Chromium, Firefox and WebKit decodes to `expected`, and strict adoption keyed by `value` binds it without a write",
    "crates/forma-ir/src/keys.rs tests: canonical_key_text over `values` and `collisions`, canonical_list_key over `paths` (JSON through json_to_slot_value)",
    "packages/core/src/dom/__tests__/key-text.test.ts: canonicalKeyText over `values` and `collisions`, a property read over `paths`"
  ],
  "note": "Hand-enumerated finite vectors; not a generator. A lone UTF-16 surrogate cannot be a vector: serde_json refuses the file and Rust strings cannot hold one, so core's unpaired-surrogate refusal is pinned in its own test only; an escaped surrogate pair is an ordinary supplementary character. Both readers parse every number lexeme here to the same double: 9007199254740992 is 2^53 in both, 9007199254740993 rounds to 2^53 in both (so it refuses as beyond the bound, which is the lexeme's meaning too), 1e3 and 9007199254740991.0 are exact integers, and the float_roundtrip feature keeps 1.5 and 1e300 exact. -0 and -0.0 parse to a zero that prints 0. Values JSON cannot spell (NaN, the infinities, undefined, a Date, a bigint) are pinned in each side's own tests.",
  "values": [
    { "id": "empty-string", "value": "", "expected": "", "served": "" },
    { "id": "ascii", "value": "row-1", "expected": "row-1", "served": "row-1" },
    { "id": "unicode", "value": "Zoë ✓ 😀", "expected": "Zoë ✓ 😀", "served": "Zoë ✓ 😀" },
    { "id": "escaped-surrogate-pair", "value": "\ud83d\ude00", "expected": "\ud83d\ude00", "served": "😀" },
